import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"ADR-0072: Concurrent Arrow Calculus","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"handl/project/adrs/0072-concurrent-arrow-calculus.md","filePath":"vendored/handl/project/adrs/0072-concurrent-arrow-calculus.md"}`),a={name:`handl/project/adrs/0072-concurrent-arrow-calculus.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="adr-0072-concurrent-arrow-calculus" tabindex="-1">ADR-0072: Concurrent Arrow Calculus <a class="header-anchor" href="#adr-0072-concurrent-arrow-calculus" aria-label="Permalink to “ADR-0072: Concurrent Arrow Calculus”">​</a></h1><h2 id="status" tabindex="-1">Status <a class="header-anchor" href="#status" aria-label="Permalink to “Status”">​</a></h2><p>Accepted</p><p>Builds on <a href="./0070-refinement-inference-scope.html">ADR-0070 — Refinement Inference Scope</a>.</p><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to “Context”">​</a></h2><p>The arrow calculus is deterministic dataflow: given an input, the graph produces a unique output. The inference mechanism reads the proof off the graph structure, which requires that the structure fully determines the behavior.</p><p>Concurrency appears to break this assumption:</p><ul><li><strong>Non-deterministic scheduling</strong>: the order of operations is not determined by the graph</li><li><strong>Race conditions</strong>: multiple operations on shared state, order-dependent</li><li><strong>Lock ordering</strong>: deadlock freedom depends on acquisition order</li><li><strong>Channel communication</strong>: message ordering and buffering</li></ul><p>The question is whether the current 5 combinators (Seq, First, Choice, Loop, App) can express concurrent systems, or whether new primitives (<code>Parallel</code>, <code>Race</code>, <code>Channel</code>, <code>Lock</code>) are required.</p><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to “Decision”">​</a></h2><p>Concurrency is expressible within the existing calculus. The key insight: <strong>externalize the non-determinism</strong>. The scheduler is modeled as part of the input (an event stream), not as part of the graph. The graph is deterministic; the non-determinism is in which events arrive.</p><h3 id="core-model" tabindex="-1">Core model <a class="header-anchor" href="#core-model" aria-label="Permalink to “Core model”">​</a></h3><p>A concurrent system is a loop over an event stream:</p><div class="language-"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>concurrent_system = loop(comp(handle_event, first(id)))</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Events arrive via the input stream. The shared state is the feedback channel <code>C</code>. <code>handle_event</code> uses <code>left</code> (Choice) to branch over event types. The graph itself is deterministic dataflow — given a specific event, it produces a specific state transition.</p><p>The &quot;all possible interleavings&quot; question becomes: <strong>what invariant holds for ALL possible event streams?</strong> This is exactly what the fixpoint computes. Because <code>handle_event</code> branches over all event types via Choice, the fixpoint discovers invariants that hold regardless of which branch is taken — i.e., regardless of event ordering.</p><p>This is the same mechanism as state machines (ADR-0073): the invariant is discovered from the loop body&#39;s guarantee via fixpoint iteration. The only difference is that the &quot;state machine&quot; here has event-driven transitions, and the events are externally supplied.</p><h3 id="shared-memory-concurrency" tabindex="-1">Shared-memory concurrency <a class="header-anchor" href="#shared-memory-concurrency" aria-label="Permalink to “Shared-memory concurrency”">​</a></h3><p>Model shared state as the feedback channel <code>C</code>. Multiple &quot;threads&quot; are different event handlers in a <code>left</code> (Choice) — each event type corresponds to a thread&#39;s operation on the shared state. The interleaving is in the event stream (external non-determinism), not in the graph.</p><p>The invariant on <code>C</code> is what the fixpoint discovers. &quot;Race condition safety&quot; means the invariant holds for all event orderings — if the fixpoint finds an invariant that is preserved by every event handler, then no interleaving can violate it.</p><h3 id="message-passing-concurrency-csp-actor" tabindex="-1">Message-passing concurrency (CSP/actor) <a class="header-anchor" href="#message-passing-concurrency-csp-actor" aria-label="Permalink to “Message-passing concurrency (CSP/actor)”">​</a></h3><p>Channel state is the feedback channel <code>C</code>. <code>receive</code> and <code>send</code> are atoms with contracts:</p><ul><li><code>receive</code> requires: channel non-empty (precondition)</li><li><code>send</code> guarantees: channel non-empty after (postcondition)</li></ul><p>The fixpoint discovers channel invariants (e.g., &quot;the channel is never empty when we try to receive&quot;). This stays within the deterministic dataflow model — the non-determinism is in message arrival, not in the graph structure.</p><h3 id="deadlock-freedom" tabindex="-1">Deadlock freedom <a class="header-anchor" href="#deadlock-freedom" aria-label="Permalink to “Deadlock freedom”">​</a></h3><p>If the loop body&#39;s atoms have preconditions (e.g., <code>receive</code> requires a non-empty channel), the fixpoint discovers the invariant that ensures the precondition is always met. &quot;Deadlock freedom&quot; means the discovered invariant implies all preconditions are satisfied for every event in the stream.</p><h3 id="inference-implications" tabindex="-1">Inference implications <a class="header-anchor" href="#inference-implications" aria-label="Permalink to “Inference implications”">​</a></h3><p>No new inference rules are required. The fixpoint computation is unchanged from ADR-0073: it iterates over the loop body&#39;s guarantee until it reaches a fixed point. The only difference is that the loop body contains a Choice over event types, so the fixpoint must find an invariant that is preserved by every branch — which is exactly what it already does for any Choice.</p><p>WP branching through Choice handles the disjunction: each event handler branch contributes its guarantee, and the fixpoint finds the invariant that all branches preserve.</p><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to “Consequences”">​</a></h2><h3 id="positive" tabindex="-1">Positive <a class="header-anchor" href="#positive" aria-label="Permalink to “Positive”">​</a></h3><ul><li>No new calculus primitives — concurrency uses only Seq, First, Choice/left, Loop, App</li><li>No new inference rules — the fixpoint mechanism from ADR-0073 applies directly</li><li>Shared-state invariants are discoverable (race condition safety)</li><li>Channel invariants are discoverable (message-passing safety)</li><li>Deadlock freedom is provable via precondition preservation</li><li>The graph remains deterministic dataflow, fully within ADR-0070&#39;s scope — the non-determinism is external (in the event stream), not internal (in the graph)</li></ul><h3 id="negative" tabindex="-1">Negative <a class="header-anchor" href="#negative" aria-label="Permalink to “Negative”">​</a></h3><ul><li>Liveness properties (temporal, not invariant) are not provable — the fixpoint discovers invariants, not temporal properties like &quot;eventually a response is sent&quot;</li><li>Global properties spanning multiple independent loops are not provable — the fixpoint is per-loop, so invariants that require reasoning about two or more concurrent loops together are out of reach</li><li>The model requires the user to externalize the scheduler as an event stream, which is a different programming style than direct concurrency primitives</li></ul><h2 id="status-1" tabindex="-1">Status <a class="header-anchor" href="#status-1" aria-label="Permalink to “Status”">​</a></h2><p>Accepted. Concurrency is in scope, modeled as dataflow over event streams. The scheduler is part of the input, not part of the graph. The fixpoint discovers invariants that hold for all event orderings, using the same mechanism as state machines (ADR-0073). Liveness properties and global multi-loop properties remain out of scope.</p><h2 id="related-adrs" tabindex="-1">Related ADRs <a class="header-anchor" href="#related-adrs" aria-label="Permalink to “Related ADRs”">​</a></h2><ul><li><a href="./0070-refinement-inference-scope.html">ADR-0070 — Refinement Inference Scope</a></li><li><a href="./0073-state-machines-as-loops.html">ADR-0073 — State Machines as Loops</a></li><li><a href="./0068-predicates-as-types.html">ADR-0068 — Predicates as Types</a></li><li><a href="./0069-modules.html">ADR-0069 — Modules</a></li><li><a href="./0071-failure-tracking-arrows.html">ADR-0071 — Failure-Tracking Arrows</a></li></ul>`,38)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};